Subject: Per-runtime fact groups with watchpoints in the lowest-level mutators, the eval scan, and one runtime per compiled unit

What this does
  - Fact groups (`qjs_aot_fact_new/expect/expect_proto/validate/depend/break`, declared in quickjs.h): per runtime, one group per watched object, naming its [[prototype]], the values of listed own data properties, and with CLOSED no other own property. Three states (PENDING, VALID, BROKEN, no re-promotion). A dependent is a word and a mask in the caller's statics: the bit is set while VALID and cleared on break. FRESH: a construct of the object while the group is PENDING breaks it.
  - Watching: a group flags its object `gv_watched` and marks it a prototype. The mutators that already test `gv_watched` now also report to the groups: `add_shape_property`, `add_property`, `js_shape_prepare_update` (delete, attribute change, redefinition), `js_aot_content_store` (every store of a value into an existing slot, from the interpreter's direct stores, the AOT helpers, `JS_SetPropertyInternal` and the define path), the IC add transitions, `JS_SetPrototypeInternal` and `js_create_from_ctor_cap`. Bytecode, builtins (Object.assign, defineProperty(ies), Reflect.*, freeze/seal, spread), the C API and native modules all pass them.
  - The eval scan (`JS_EvalFunctionInternal`, so eval, indirect eval, `new Function`, runtime modules and host `JS_Eval` all pass): before new code runs, its register program (nested functions included) is read for the atoms its stores name and for stores whose key it does not spell (computed keys, spread, delete, `with`, prototype changes, nested eval). A group that lists a written atom, and every group on a dynamic write, is broken before the code runs. Read-only code breaks nothing.
  - One runtime per unit: the first runtime to bind a function owns the compiled unit (`js_aot_unit_owner`, a compare-exchange); another runtime binds nothing and interprets, until the owner is freed. The unit carries `qjs_aot_bundle_tag` (a hash of its function digests), recorded in the runtime that owns it. `qjs_aot_runtime_bound(rt)` and `qjs_aot_runtime_bundle(rt)` report them.
  - `QJS_KIT_TEST_KNOBS` builds read `QJS_AOT_FACTS_OFF=add,store,edit,proto,construct,eval,all` (a mechanism switched off, for the verification kit's negative controls) and `QJS_AOT_UNIT_SHARED=1` (no ownership).

Why we need it
  A compiled body that was built on facts about prototypes, method identities and layouts has to leave when one of them breaks, whatever broke it. Today's invalidation is a runtime-wide epoch and a bit per contracted layout; the new backend (AOT-REDESIGN.md R2 on) needs groups it can depend on one by one, with a validity word per function, per runtime (the old statics were process-global), and it needs every mutation path covered, which only the lowest-level mutators do. The unit's per-function statics are not safe to share between runtimes, hence the owner.

Soundness
  Nothing consumes the groups yet, so generated code is unchanged (deltablue C.Minstr identical). The events fire before the mutation they report and only set a state and clear dependent bits: they allocate nothing, free nothing and call no JS. A broken group keeps its references until the next group is made or the runtime is freed (freeing in a mutator could run a finalizer). The scan only ever breaks more groups than the hooks would, never fewer.

Functions touched
  `add_shape_property`, `add_property`, `js_shape_prepare_update`, `js_aot_content_store`, the two IC add paths, `JS_SetPrototypeInternal`, `js_create_from_ctor_cap`, `JS_EvalFunctionInternal`, `js_aot_bind`, `JS_FreeRuntime`, and new: `js_aot_obj_event`, `js_aot_fact_*`, `js_aot_scan_bytecode`, `js_aot_facts_eval_scan`, `js_aot_unit_claim/release`, `qjs_aot_runtime_bound/bundle`.

No.

